# Drizzle ORM Best Practices

This document outlines best practices for using Drizzle ORM in TypeScript projects based on community standards and official recommendations.

## Schema Organization

### Organize Schema by Domain

```typescript
// 📁 src/db/schema/users.ts
export const users = pgTable('users', { /* ... */ });
export const usersRelations = relations(users, ({ many }) => ({ /* ... */ }));

// 📁 src/db/schema/posts.ts
export const posts = pgTable('posts', { /* ... */ });
export const postsRelations = relations(posts, ({ one }) => ({ /* ... */ }));

// 📁 src/db/schema/index.ts
export * from './users';
export * from './posts';
```

When your application grows, organize schema files by domain or feature rather than keeping everything in a single file.

### Use Consistent Naming Conventions

- Table names: Plural nouns (`users`, `posts`, `order_items`)
- Column names: Prefer snake_case in the database
- TypeScript identifiers: Use camelCase
- Relations: Name according to their semantic meaning

```typescript
// Table names are plural
export const users = pgTable('users', {
  // Column names in snake_case
  id: serial('id').primaryKey(),
  firstName: text('first_name').notNull(),
  createdAt: timestamp('created_at').defaultNow()
});
```

### Export Schema from a Central Location

Create an index file that exports all schema components to make imports cleaner and enable Drizzle Kit to find all your tables.

```typescript
// 📁 src/db/schema/index.ts
export * from './users';
export * from './posts';
export * from './comments';

// 📁 src/db/index.ts
import * as schema from './schema';
import { drizzle } from 'drizzle-orm/node-postgres';

export const db = drizzle(client, { schema });
```

## Type Safety

### Leverage Inferred Types

Drizzle automatically infers types from your schema definitions. Use these types for better type safety.

```typescript
import { InferSelectModel, InferInsertModel } from 'drizzle-orm';
import { users } from '../db/schema';

// Types inferred from schema
type User = InferSelectModel<typeof users>;
type NewUser = InferInsertModel<typeof users>;

function createUser(user: NewUser): Promise<User> {
  // Implementation
}
```

### Use Schema Validation with zod/typebox

Combine Drizzle with schema validation libraries for runtime validation.

```typescript
import { createInsertSchema } from 'drizzle-zod';
import { users } from '../db/schema';

// Create zod schema from Drizzle schema
const insertUserSchema = createInsertSchema(users);

// Validate data before insertion
function createUser(data: unknown) {
  const validatedData = insertUserSchema.parse(data);
  return db.insert(users).values(validatedData);
}
```

## Relations

### Define Explicit Relations

Always define explicit relations between tables to enable relational queries.

```typescript
import { relations } from 'drizzle-orm';

export const posts = pgTable('posts', {
  id: serial('id').primaryKey(),
  title: text('title').notNull(),
  authorId: integer('author_id').references(() => users.id)
});

// Define relations explicitly
export const postsRelations = relations(posts, ({ one }) => ({
  author: one(users, {
    fields: [posts.authorId],
    references: [users.id]
  })
}));

export const usersRelations = relations(users, ({ many }) => ({
  posts: many(posts)
}));
```

### Use Relational Queries

When fetching related data, use Drizzle's relational query API for optimal performance.

```typescript
// Fetches all posts with their authors in a single query
const postsWithAuthors = await db.query.posts.findMany({
  with: {
    author: true
  }
});

// Select only specific fields
const postsWithAuthorNames = await db.query.posts.findMany({
  columns: {
    id: true,
    title: true
  },
  with: {
    author: {
      columns: {
        name: true
      }
    }
  }
});
```

## Migrations

### Choose the Right Migration Strategy

Drizzle supports multiple migration approaches. Choose the one that best fits your workflow:

1. **SQL Migrations** (recommended for production): Generate and version migration files
   ```bash
   npx drizzle-kit generate
   ```

2. **Push Migrations** (good for development): Direct schema changes to the database
   ```bash
   npx drizzle-kit push
   ```

3. **Pull Strategy** (for existing databases): Generate schema from database
   ```bash
   npx drizzle-kit pull
   ```

### Keep Migrations Atomic

Each migration should focus on a single logical change and should include both `up` and `down` migrations for reversibility.

```typescript
// 📁 migrations/0001_create_users.ts
export async function up(db) {
  await db.execute(sql`
    CREATE TABLE users (
      id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
      email TEXT UNIQUE NOT NULL,
      created_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
    );
  `);
}

export async function down(db) {
  await db.execute(sql`DROP TABLE IF EXISTS users;`);
}
```

### Version Control Migration Files

Always commit migration files to version control and ensure all team members apply migrations in the same order.

### Use Meaningful Migration Names

Name migration files descriptively so their purpose is clear:

```
20231215_create_users_table.sql
20231216_add_profile_fields.sql
20231217_create_posts_table.sql
```

## Query Performance

### Use Prepared Statements for Repeated Queries

For queries that run frequently with different parameters, use prepared statements.

```typescript
// Prepare once
const getUserById = db.query.users.findFirst({
  where: (users, { eq }) => eq(users.id, sql.placeholder('id')),
  with: {
    posts: true
  }
}).prepare();

// Execute multiple times with different values
const user1 = await getUserById.execute({ id: 1 });
const user2 = await getUserById.execute({ id: 2 });
```

### Select Only Needed Columns

Avoid selecting all columns when you only need a subset.

```typescript
// ❌ Selecting all columns
const allUsers = await db.select().from(users);

// ✅ Selecting only needed columns
const userNames = await db.select({
  id: users.id,
  name: users.name
}).from(users);
```

### Use Transactions for Related Operations

Wrap related database operations in transactions to ensure consistency.

```typescript
await db.transaction(async (tx) => {
  const [user] = await tx.insert(users).values({
    name: 'John',
    email: 'john@example.com'
  }).returning();
  
  await tx.insert(posts).values({
    title: 'Hello World',
    authorId: user.id
  });
});
```

### Employ Pagination

For large datasets, always implement pagination to limit result size.

```typescript
const pageSize = 20;
const page = 1;

const paginatedUsers = await db.select()
  .from(users)
  .limit(pageSize)
  .offset((page - 1) * pageSize);
```

## Error Handling

### Catch and Handle Database-Specific Errors

Identify and handle database-specific errors appropriately.

```typescript
import { UniqueConstraintViolationError } from 'drizzle-orm/pg-core';

try {
  await db.insert(users).values({
    email: 'existing@example.com'
  });
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof UniqueConstraintViolationError) {
    // Handle duplicate email
    return { error: 'Email already exists' };
  }
  // Handle other errors
  throw error;
}
```

### Use Default Values and NOT NULL Constraints

Define sensible defaults and NOT NULL constraints in your schema to prevent data integrity issues.

```typescript
export const users = pgTable('users', {
  id: serial('id').primaryKey(),
  name: text('name').notNull(),
  role: text('role').notNull().default('user'),
  createdAt: timestamp('created_at').defaultNow().notNull()
});
```

## Configuration

### Use Environment-Specific Configuration

Configure Drizzle differently for development, testing, and production environments.

```typescript
// 📁 drizzle.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from 'drizzle-kit';
import dotenv from 'dotenv';

// Load environment-specific .env file
dotenv.config({ path: `.env.${process.env.NODE_ENV}` });

export default defineConfig({
  dialect: 'postgresql',
  schema: './src/db/schema',
  out: './migrations',
  dbCredentials: {
    host: process.env.DB_HOST,
    user: process.env.DB_USER,
    password: process.env.DB_PASSWORD,
    database: process.env.DB_NAME
  }
});
```

### Separate Database Connection Logic

Keep database connection logic separate from schema definitions.

```typescript
// 📁 src/db/connection.ts
import { drizzle } from 'drizzle-orm/node-postgres';
import { Pool } from 'pg';
import * as schema from './schema';

const pool = new Pool({
  host: process.env.DB_HOST,
  user: process.env.DB_USER,
  password: process.env.DB_PASSWORD,
  database: process.env.DB_NAME
});

export const db = drizzle(pool, { schema });
```

## Testing

### Use SQLite for Tests

For faster tests, consider using SQLite in memory during testing.

```typescript
// 📁 src/db/test-utils.ts
import { drizzle } from 'drizzle-orm/better-sqlite3';
import Database from 'better-sqlite3';
import * as schema from './schema';
import { migrate } from 'drizzle-orm/better-sqlite3/migrator';

export function createTestDatabase() {
  const sqlite = new Database(':memory:');
  const db = drizzle(sqlite, { schema });
  
  // Apply migrations
  migrate(db, { migrationsFolder: './migrations' });
  
  return { db, sqlite };
}
```

### Isolate Test Data

For each test, set up isolated test data and clean up afterward.

```typescript
import { beforeEach, afterEach, test } from 'vitest';
import { createTestDatabase } from './db/test-utils';

let db;
let sqlite;

beforeEach(() => {
  const testDb = createTestDatabase();
  db = testDb.db;
  sqlite = testDb.sqlite;
});

afterEach(() => {
  sqlite.close();
});

test('should create a user', async () => {
  const [user] = await db.insert(users)
    .values({ name: 'Test User', email: 'test@example.com' })
    .returning();
    
  expect(user.name).toBe('Test User');
});
```

## Performance Optimization

### Use Batch Operations

For bulk operations, use batch inserts/updates to improve performance.

```typescript
// Insert multiple records in one query
await db.insert(users).values([
  { name: 'User 1', email: 'user1@example.com' },
  { name: 'User 2', email: 'user2@example.com' },
  { name: 'User 3', email: 'user3@example.com' }
]);
```

### Consider Using Materialized Views

For complex reports or analytics queries, consider using materialized views.

```typescript
await db.execute(sql`
  CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW user_stats AS
  SELECT 
    u.id,
    u.name,
    COUNT(p.id) AS post_count
  FROM users u
  LEFT JOIN posts p ON p.author_id = u.id
  GROUP BY u.id, u.name;
`);
```

### Implement Connection Pooling

For production applications, use connection pooling to manage database connections efficiently.

```typescript
import { Pool } from 'pg';
import { drizzle } from 'drizzle-orm/node-postgres';

const pool = new Pool({
  max: 10, // Maximum number of clients
  idleTimeoutMillis: 30000, // How long a client is allowed to remain idle before being closed
  connectionTimeoutMillis: 2000 // How long to wait for a connection
});

const db = drizzle(pool);
```

## Security

### Never Trust User Input

Always validate user input before using it in queries.

```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';

const userSchema = z.object({
  name: z.string().min(1).max(100),
  email: z.string().email()
});

app.post('/users', async (req, res) => {
  try {
    const userData = userSchema.parse(req.body);
    const newUser = await db.insert(users).values(userData).returning();
    res.json(newUser);
  } catch (error) {
    res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid input' });
  }
});
```

### Use the node: Protocol for Imports

For built-in Node.js modules, use the node: protocol to prevent potential typosquatting attacks.

```typescript
// ❌ Avoid
import { createHash } from 'crypto';

// ✅ Better
import { createHash } from 'node:crypto';
```

## Project Structure

### Follow a Domain-Driven Structure

Organize your database code according to domains.

```
src/
├── db/
│   ├── schema/
│   │   ├── index.ts
│   │   ├── users.ts
│   │   ├── posts.ts
│   │   └── comments.ts
│   ├── migrations/
│   ├── repositories/
│   │   ├── userRepository.ts
│   │   └── postRepository.ts
│   ├── connection.ts
│   └── index.ts
├── features/
│   ├── users/
│   └── posts/
└── index.ts
```

### Consider Implementing Repository Pattern

Use the repository pattern to encapsulate database queries and provide a clean API.

```typescript
// 📁 src/db/repositories/userRepository.ts
import { db } from '../connection';
import { users } from '../schema';
import type { NewUser, User } from '../types';

export const userRepository = {
  findById: async (id: number): Promise<User | undefined> => {
    return db.query.users.findFirst({
      where: (users, { eq }) => eq(users.id, id)
    });
  },
  
  create: async (userData: NewUser): Promise<User> => {
    const [user] = await db.insert(users).values(userData).returning();
    return user;
  },
  
  // Other methods...
};
```

## CI/CD Integration

### Automate Migrations in Deployment Pipeline

Integrate database migrations into your CI/CD pipeline.

```yaml
# .github/workflows/deploy.yml
name: Deploy

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]

jobs:
  deploy:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v3
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v3
        with:
          node-version: '18'
      - run: npm ci
      - name: Run database migrations
        run: npx drizzle-kit migrate
        env:
          DATABASE_URL: ${{ secrets.DATABASE_URL }}
      # Deployment steps...
```

### Use Database Snapshots for Reviews

Generate and review migration SQL files in pull requests.

```yaml
# .github/workflows/pr-check.yml
name: PR Check

on: [pull_request]

jobs:
  db-check:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v3
      - uses: actions/setup-node@v3
        with:
          node-version: '18'
      - run: npm ci
      - name: Generate migration files
        run: npx drizzle-kit generate
      - name: Check for changes in migration files
        run: |
          if [ -n "$(git status --porcelain migrations)" ]; then
            echo "New migrations detected. Please review the changes."
            git diff --staged migrations
            exit 1
          fi
```

